---
import { MethodStrip } from '@/components/site/Method';
---

<MethodStrip of="checkers" />
<p>
  Every system answers every case once. A case passes only when its response passes every applicable
  deterministic rule. Empty, incomplete and errored responses fail every applicable rule. Prose
  cases also require authored prose. Rule diagnostics report their applicable case and response
  denominators separately from the strict score.
</p>
<p>
  The format checks reject Unicode ellipses and curly English quotes in authored prose, and
  halfwidth commas, periods and colons between Chinese characters in the same prose span, ignoring
  whitespace. They also check one physical source line per Markdown paragraph, list spacing, aligned
  directory-tree comments and omission of a planted marker. Code, link destinations and exact
  declared input quotations are excluded from prose checks. Markdown and text fences are parsed
  recursively.
</p>
<p>
  A list with only single-paragraph items stays tight. If any item contains another paragraph, a
  nested list or a code block, every item and each nested part is separated by a blank line. Tree
  comments share a source column, with at least two comments and three spaces after each path. These
  checks make no judgment about meaning, tone or factual accuracy.
</p>
<p>
  Pass rates use the case as the sampling unit, with exact 95% Clopper-Pearson intervals. The leader
  comparison uses the same exact paired sign test as the tests scorer. Systems the test cannot
  separate remain tied.
</p>
<p>
  Prompt effects compare each bare system with its instructed counterpart on the same cases. The
  only generation difference is the added instruction prompt. Each effect is the mean
  prompted-minus-bare binary case score, with a paired 95% percentile bootstrap interval when the
  observed differences vary. An interval is absent when variation cannot be estimated. This is a
  descriptive comparison.
</p>
